<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Islamic Golden Age</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Abbasid Golden Age, Golden Age of Islam</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Islamic Golden Age refers to a period of flourishing science, philosophy, literature and art in the Islamic world, conventionally associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> from the eighth century. Scholars in centres such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> translated and extended Greek, Persian and Indian learning. Central Asian cities later ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> had been important centres of this tradition.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The period is dated from the 8th century CE to the 14th century and is understood to have begun under the Abbasid caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> (786–809), with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom">House of Wisdom</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, one of the world's largest cities at the time. The institution drew scholars from across the Muslim world to translate the classical knowledge of the known world into Arabic.</p><p>Several causes are proposed. Quranic injunctions valuing education and knowledge may have encouraged the search for learning, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> attributed to Muhammad states that seeking knowledge is obligatory upon every Muslim. The state patronised scholars heavily. Paper spread from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> into Muslim regions in the eighth century through mass production in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, which made it possible for the first time to make a living from writing and selling books.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Intellectual and cultural activity flourished in many centres besides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, Umayyad Córdoba, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>, and later Nasrid Granada. The period is said to have ended with the collapse of the Abbasid caliphate after the Mongol invasions and the Siege of Baghdad in 1258, though some scholars extend it to around 1350, to the 15th century or even the 16th.</p><p>Muḥammad ibn Mūsā al-Khwārizmī contributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a>, arithmetic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>–Arabic numerals, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omar-khayyam">Omar Khayyam</a> found the general geometric solution of the cubic equation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-haytham">Ibn al-Haytham</a>, known as Alhazen, argued correctly that vision occurs when light travelling in straight lines reflects off an object into the eyes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-biruni">Al-Biruni</a> (973–1050) estimated the radius of the earth at 6339.6 km, the best estimate of his time. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">Calligraphy</a>, geometric ornament and the poetry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez">Hafez</a>, Saadi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdowsi">Ferdowsi</a> flourished.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The metaphor of a golden age was applied to Islamic history in 19th-century literature, in the context of the western fashion known as Orientalism. Translations of philosophical texts from Arabic into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> led, according to the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, to the transformation of almost all philosophical disciplines in the medieval Latin world.</p><p>Explanations of the decline differ. The economic historian Joel Mokyr has argued that al-Ghazali was a key figure in the decline of Islamic science, while George Saliba points out that the golden age did not slow after al-Ghazali and places the golden age of astronomy in the period after him. Ahmad Y. al-Hassan holds that science declined only after the 16th century and explains the decline by economic and political factors.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Islamic Golden Age” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The learning of the <b>Abbasid Golden Age</b> was recovered through Arabic translations in Sicily and Spain, and later European science was built in part upon it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Conquered by the Arabs in the eighth century, Transoxiana became a focal point of the <b>Islamic Golden Age</b> under the Samanids, was razed by the Mongols in the thirteenth century and flowered again under Timur, whose Samarkand became a centre of science under his grandson Ulugh Beg.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>Islamic Golden Age</b> Arabs promoted free trade and banking, and their Indo-Arabic numerals, which eased bookkeeping, reached Europe through cities such as Venice and Pisa.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
